#
# prima-materia — Syriac asceticism
#
# Opened from the structure-driven entrance of SPEC §14: registered at
# pm:corpusNamed in traditions/registry-mediterranean.ttl with no node. What
# was opened is the first of the three works the registration names, the
# First Part of Isaac of Nineveh in Bedjan's Syriac, and not the whole of it:
# five of its eighty-two discourses were read complete — XIX on revelations,
# XXXV the long treatise in questions and answers, XXXVI on the ways Satan
# makes war, LX against asking for signs, LXX on judging one's degree by the
# states of the mind — chosen because a search of the whole translation for
# signs, tokens, tears, miracles and terms put most of the passages that could
# carry a sign or a failure there. The other seventy-seven discourses are
# unread and the work node says so.
#
# Order of the file: the works; the tradition with the three decisions; the
# practices and what they are said to bring about; the states the text gives
# as reachable and the signs it gives of them; the prerequisites; the
# conditions under which the text says the practice does not work; the
# cautions; the powers it reports and tells the reader not to ask for.
#
# Citation. Every node cites the Syriac by discourse and page of Bedjan, the
# page numbers Wensinck prints in his margin and the Syriac file prints in its
# running heads; and two translations by two translators: A. J. Wensinck's
# from Bedjan's Syriac, by page, and the Holy Transfiguration Monastery's from
# the Greek and the Syriac, by homily and page. The two number the discourses
# differently, because the second follows the order of the Greek version: XXXV
# is its Homily 37, XXXVI its Homilies 38 and 39, LX its Homily 60, LXX its
# Homily 68.
#

#
# The works
#

pmw:IsaacNinevehFirstPart a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Isaac of Nineveh, the First Part, the Ascetical Homilies"@en, "Isaak von Ninive, der Erste Teil, die asketischen Reden"@de ;
    skos:note "Read on 2026-10-20 in the Syriac of Paul Bedjan's edition of the First Part, Mar Isaacus Ninivita, De perfectione religiosa (1909), from archive.org 212102178MarIsaacusNinivitaDePerfectioneReligiosa, a vocalised Syriac text layer of 456613 Syriac letters against none Latin, searched after the vowel points were stripped. The Latin title page is not in the text layer; that this is Bedjan's text rests on its running heads, which carry the discourse number and the page, and on those pages agreeing passage for passage with the Bedjan pages Wensinck prints in his margin. The Syriac was read at the two passages on tears, XXXV pp. 245-247, at the angel of light, XXXVI p. 277, at LX pp. 421-423 and at LXX p. 488, where the translations divide; the other passages entered rest on the two translations and on Bedjan's pagination as Wensinck prints it, and XIX was not compared."@en ;
    skos:note "Two translations by two translators. A. J. Wensinck, Mystic Treatises by Isaac of Nineveh, translated from Bedjan's Syriac text (Verhandelingen der Koninklijke Akademie van Wetenschappen te Amsterdam, Afdeeling Letterkunde, Nieuwe Reeks XXIII.1, Amsterdam), archive.org isaac_of_nineveh_mystical_treatises; the title page in the text layer reads 1933 by OCR and the running footer of the volume reads 1922, so the year is not settled from the text and is given in the citations without one. The Holy Transfiguration Monastery, The Ascetical Homilies of Saint Isaac the Syrian, revised second edition, translated from the Greek and Syriac (Boston: Holy Transfiguration Monastery, 2020), archive.org the-ascetical-homilies-of-saint-isaac-the-syrian-holy-transfiguration-monastery, the title page in the full text. Sebastian Brock's The Wisdom of St Isaac of Nineveh (Piscataway: Gorgias 2006) was also opened; it is a short anthology and contains none of the passages entered here."@en ;
    skos:note "Read complete: discourses XIX, XXXV, XXXVI, LXX in Wensinck, and LX in Wensinck to p. 284 and in the Holy Transfiguration translation to p. 434. Unread: the other seventy-seven discourses of the First Part, among them LI on prayer and LXIV on the many varying states, which by their headings bear on what the registration names. The Second Part, which Bedjan does not print, was not looked for."@en .

pmw:JohnOfDalyathaLetters a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
